---
/**
 * /vs-monte-carlo/ — the first question every reviewer asks.
 *
 * The honest thesis is composition, not replacement. Monte Carlo dispersion is
 * a first-class phase chain in this crate, the shipped examples use it, and the
 * project's own tutorial records the ensemble overturning a false single-draw
 * finding. A page that argued the fork replaces the ensemble would be arguing
 * against the crate's own examples.
 *
 * The centrepiece is the retropulsion roster's `dv m/s` and `dv frozen`
 * columns, because they are the coupled answer and the decoupled answer to the
 * same intervention, printed side by side by the run itself.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/nav/SiteHeader.astro';
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import { rustDark, rustLight } from '../../../shiki-rust-themes.mjs';

const SHIKI = { light: rustLight, dark: rustDark };

const title = 'Fork or sweep — DeepCausality CFD';
const description =
  'A dispersion campaign varies inputs at t = 0 and composes afterwards. A state fork pauses one coupled run and continues every alternative from it. Both ship here, they answer different questions, and the retropulsion roster prints the difference as two columns.';

/* weather/main.rs:72-84. */
const ensemble = `let table = CfdFlow::study("weather-dispersion table")
    .cases(model::weather_cases())
    .baseline(model::standard_day)      // the validated origin, built once
    .alternate(model::weather_world)    // six counterfactual atmospheres, each marked
    .ensemble(constants::MC_DRAWS)      // deterministic receiver-noise draws
    .couple(|case, draw| world::corridor_coupling(model::bias_departure(case.d_temp), draw))
    .march_for(constants::STEPS, world::initial_field)   // from a fresh initial field
    .reduce_ensemble(model::world_row)  // draw sets collapse to mean / scatter / worst
    .gates(model::weather_gates())
    .verdict()?;`;

/* corridor/main.rs:98-115, condensed. */
const fork = `let corridor = CfdFlow::study("bank-angle corridor")
    .cases(model::coarse_commands())
    .fork(&onset)                       // one paused, marched, coupled state
    .branch(model::bank_world)          // one alternated world per command
    .continue_for(constants::BRANCH_STEPS)
    .reduce_all(model::score_branches)
    .gates(model::corridor_gates())
    .verdict()?;`;

/* retropulsion/output.txt:24-29, verbatim. */
const roster = `  branch         cmd    flown  preserved  axial m/s2    prop kg     dv m/s  dv frozen
  coast         0.00   0.0000          —     10.5950       1.81    139.519      0.144
  low           0.20   0.2000     0.2510      7.4718      62.05     88.399     49.490
  mid           0.40   0.4000     0.1238      9.9667     122.30    120.335     99.984
  high          0.60   0.6000    -0.0161     12.8932     182.54    151.991    151.413
  hard          0.85   0.7931    -0.0611     17.1977     256.80    212.085    216.209`;

const compare = [
  {
    q: 'What varies',
    sweep: 'An input, before the run starts.',
    forkc: 'The world a branch continues in, at a pause the flow chose.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What each branch starts from',
    sweep: 'A fresh initial field. Branches share a description, not a past.',
    forkc: 'The marched coupled field as it stands. Branches share a past.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What it answers',
    sweep: 'How wide is the outcome, given what I do not know.',
    forkc: 'Which command should I commit, given where the vehicle actually is.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What it costs',
    sweep: 'One full march per case per draw.',
    forkc: 'One march to the pause, then a continuation per branch.',
  },
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path="/vs-monte-carlo/">
  <SiteHeader slot="header" />

  <article class="page mpage">
    <header class="mhead">
      <p class="eyebrow">Fork or sweep</p>
      <h1>Where a dispersion campaign stops</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Both ship here, and the shipped examples use both. They answer different
        questions, and the retropulsion run prints the difference as two columns
        of one table.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="mgroup">
      <h2>The distinction</h2>
      <p class="body">
        A parameter sweep re-runs a model from different inputs, and each branch
        is an independent world. A state fork pauses one marched coupled field
        and continues every branch from it, so an intervention feeds back into
        the flow it inherited.
      </p>
      <p class="body">
        The rule the tutorial gives for choosing: fork a state when the branches
        must share a past, alternate a world when they must share a description
        and differ in one declared way.
      </p>

      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col"></th>
              <th scope="col">Dispersion sweep</th>
              <th scope="col">State fork</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {compare.map((r) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{r.q}</th>
                <td>{r.sweep}</td>
                <td>{r.forkc}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mgroup">
      <h2>Both are first-class</h2>
      <p class="body">
        The ensemble path is not a thing this works around. It is a phase chain
        in the same language, and the weather campaign flies 48 descents through
        it: six atmospheres, eight deterministic receiver-noise draws each, every
        one from a fresh initial field.
      </p>
      <Code code={ensemble} lang="rust" themes={SHIKI} />
      <p class="body">
        The fork path takes the same grammar and replaces the origin. Instead of
        a baseline description it takes a paused run.
      </p>
      <Code code={fork} lang="rust" themes={SHIKI} />
      <p class="body">
        Draws are deterministic low-discrepancy sequences rather than an RNG, so
        the campaign gains error bars and stays bit-reproducible at the same
        time. That is what lets a statistical gate bind on the result.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mgroup">
      <h2>What a sweep cannot see</h2>
      <p class="body">
        For every forked branch, the retropulsion descent also computes what
        thrust-only kinematics would have predicted for the same command
        schedule, with the flow closure frozen at the fork. That frozen column
        is what a campaign gets when it solves the drag model separately and
        composes afterwards. It is printed next to the coupled answer:
      </p>
      <Code code={roster} lang="text" themes={SHIKI} />
      <p class="body">
        Read the two right-hand columns. On the 20% branch the coupled run sheds
        88.399 m/s where the frozen prediction says 49.490, and the ordering
        changes with it: net deceleration is non-monotone in throttle, at its
        minimum on that branch, below what the vehicle sheds coasting. The
        preserved-drag column falls through zero as the roster hardens. A
        campaign that composed a separately-solved drag model would report the
        frozen column and miss the reversal.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mgroup">
      <h2>The case for the ensemble</h2>
      <p class="body">
        An earlier single-draw version of the weather table showed the cold-day
        row eight percent below its predicted value, which reads as a physics
        finding. The Monte Carlo mean identified it as one draw. A single run per
        condition cannot separate a mechanism from a draw, and no amount of
        forking fixes that.
      </p>
      <p class="body">
        So the two compose, and the shipped examples compose them. The weather
        table sizes a dispersion margin across 48 descents; the retropulsion
        descent then reads that table in flight and forks against it. Gate 5 of
        that run measures what the composition bought: informed and uninformed
        guidance lit the landing burn 14.06 m apart, and the informed world spent
        7.28 kg more propellant, which is the dispersion-sized reserve measured
        rather than configured.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mgroup">
      <h2>What the fork does not do</h2>
      <p class="body">
        A fork varies the world configuration, so a counterfactual about what the
        guidance believed had to be run as two separate marches, and it carries
        no alternation marker. The branch fan-out through
        <code>fork</code> / <code>branch</code> / <code>continue_for</code> also
        does not thread the audit sink, so it writes no log files; those branches
        carry their provenance in the returned report instead.
      </p>
      <p class="body">
        And the corridor, the cheapest fork to run, is the weakest evidence for
        the coupling: across its bank branches the flow observables agree to
        three digits. The retropulsion roster is where branches change the flow.
      </p>
      <p class="more">
        <a href="/blueprints/fork-a-running-simulation/">Blueprint: fork a running simulation</a>
        <a href="/tutorial/stage-2-weather/">Walk 2: one baseline, six worlds</a>
        <a href="/boundaries/">Measured boundaries</a>
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .mpage { max-width: var(--w-panel); }
  .mhead { margin-bottom: var(--space-7); }
  .mhead h1 { margin: var(--space-3) 0 var(--space-4); }
  .mhead .lede { color: var(--fg-1); max-width: var(--measure-lede); margin: 0; }

  .mgroup { margin-bottom: var(--space-7); }
  .mgroup h2 {
    font-size: var(--t-h3);
    padding-bottom: var(--space-3);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line-1);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
  }
  .body { color: var(--fg-1); font-size: var(--t-body-sm); max-width: var(--measure); margin: 0 0 var(--space-4); }

  table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: var(--t-body-sm); margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }
  th, td { text-align: left; padding: var(--space-3); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line-1); vertical-align: top; }
  thead th {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-eyebrow);
    letter-spacing: var(--ls-eyebrow);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--fg-2);
    font-weight: 400;
  }
  tbody th { font-weight: 400; color: var(--fg-0); }
  tbody td { color: var(--fg-1); }
  tbody tr:last-child th, tbody tr:last-child td { border-bottom: none; }

  .more {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-4);
    margin: 0;
    padding-top: var(--space-3);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line-1);
    font-size: var(--t-body-sm);
  }
</style>
